    @section oracle_connect_timeouts Connection establishment

    Configure Oracle Net timeouts in the connect descriptor used as the datasource's database name:
    @code
    (DESCRIPTION=(CONNECT_TIMEOUT=5)(TRANSPORT_CONNECT_TIMEOUT=3)(RETRY_COUNT=0)
      (ADDRESS_LIST=(FAILOVER=ON)(LOAD_BALANCE=OFF)
        (ADDRESS=(PROTOCOL=TCP)(HOST=db1.internal)(PORT=1521))
        (ADDRESS=(PROTOCOL=TCP)(HOST=db2.internal)(PORT=1521)))
      (CONNECT_DATA=(SERVICE_NAME=orders)))
    @endcode
    These are Oracle Net descriptor parameters, not Qore driver options. Oracle applies them per
    address/attempt; retry settings and multiple addresses can increase the total connection time.
    @note OCI does not expose nonblocking server attachment before login, and OCIBreak does not reliably
    interrupt OCIServerAttach. Configure finite Oracle Net timeouts. Pending Qore cancellation is checked
    after server attachment and session creation. Session creation registers an OCIBreak callback, and
    cancellation callbacks cannot access OCI handles after their helper has been destroyed.

    @section oracle_cancellation Cancelling statements: out-of-band break

    When the thread running a statement is cancelled, or its %Program or sandbox is interrupted, the driver
    cancels the statement with \c OCIBreak(), and the call raises \c ORA-01013.  How soon the server sees the
    break depends on Oracle Net's break mode:
    - <b>out-of-band break</b> (Oracle's default): the break is sent as TCP urgent data, which the server process
      gets at once, so every statement is cancelled at once, including one that waits on the server: for a row lock
      or another lock, in \c DBMS_SESSION.SLEEP, or for any other event
    - <b>in-band break</b>, used when the server's \c sqlnet.ora sets \c DISABLE_OOB=ON, or when the network path
      does not deliver TCP urgent data (some NAT devices, load balancers and firewalls remove it): the server sees
      the break only at its next break check or when it next reads the connection; a statement that is working is
      still cancelled promptly, but <b>a statement waiting on the server is not cancelled until its wait ends</b>,
      which for a lock held by another session may be never

    So that a blocked statement can always be cancelled (and an interface using it does not stay blocked when it
    is stopped), the database server must not set \c DISABLE_OOB=ON, and the network between the client and the
    server must pass TCP urgent data.  Some container images of Oracle Database disable out-of-band break in their
    \c sqlnet.ora (for example \c gvenzl/oracle-free); remove the setting from the server's \c sqlnet.ora to restore
    it.  To check a connection, run a statement that waits (for example
    <tt>begin dbms_session.sleep(30); end;</tt>) and cancel it: with out-of-band break it ends at once with
    \c ORA-01013, with in-band break only after the 30 seconds.


    When compiled again Qore 0.8.6+ the oracle driver support the following DBI options on each connection:
    - \c "optimal-numbers": return <tt>NUMBER</tt> types as an integer if possible, if not as an arbitrary-precision
      number
    - \c "string-numbers": return <tt>NUMBER</tt> types as strings (for backwards-compatibility)
    - \c "numeric-numbers": return <tt>NUMBER</tt> types as arbitrary-precision number values
    - \c "timezone": accepts a string argument that can be either a region name (ex: \c "Europe/Prague") or a UTC
      offset (ex: \c "+01:00") to set the server's time zone rules; this is useful if connecting to a database server
      in a different time zone.  If this option is not set then the server's time zone is assumed to be the same as
      the client's time zone; see @ref timezone.

    Options can be set in the \c Datasource or \c DatasourcePool constructors as in the following examples:
    @code{.py}
    Dataource ds("oracle:user/pass@db{numeric-numbers,timezone=Europe/Vienna}");
    DataourcePool dsp("oracle:user/pass@db%host.internal:1521{optimal-numbers}");
    @endcode

    Options are stored separately for each connection.

    @section number_options Number Options

    The number options (\c "optimal-numbers", \c "string-numbers", and \c "numeric-numbers") are all mutually-
    exclusive; setting one automatically disables the others.   These options also ignore their arguments; the last
    one set takes effect (also note that setting it with any value sets the option, even @ref False).

    The default if no other option is explicitly set is \c "optimal-numbers".  Note that this represents a change from
    previous versions where Oracle <tt>NUMBER</tt> values were returned as strings in order to avoid the loss of
    information.  To set the old behavior, set the \c "string-numbers" option when creating the \c Datasource or
    \c DatasourcePool object.
*/
